The fragments below use AVR register names from the Arduino Mega 2560. Choose the shift amount in the range 0–7.

```cpp
// (i) Sample physical pin PC5.
DDRC &= ~(1<<PC5);
unsigned char pc5 = (PINC >> PC5) & 1U;

// (ii) Set the PA3 latch; (iii) clear the PD6 latch.
PORTA |= (1<<PA3);
PORTD &= ~(1<<PD6);

// (iv) Shift the PORTB latch left by a specified amount.
#define shift (2)
PORTB = (unsigned char)(PORTB << shift);

// (v) Time a block, excluding serial output. Serial.begin(115200) goes in setup().
unsigned long start = micros();
PORTA |= (1<<PA3);  // Replace with the block to measure.
PORTD &= ~(1<<PD6);
unsigned long elapsedUs = micros() - start;
Serial.println(elapsedUs);
```

Set the relevant DDR bits when the latches must drive pins. A shift of `PORTB` updates the whole output latch. For code shorter than the time resolution of `micros()`, measure repeated iterations and divide after allowing for loop overhead, or count CPU cycles. At 16 MHz, each cycle is $62.5\,\mathrm{ns}$.

Assume a 10 Hz **toggle rate**, so the output changes state once every 100 ms. Connect an active-high LED through a resistor to PB4 (Mega D10) and ground. Use 50 ms instead if 10 Hz is intended as the full on/off cycle frequency.

```cpp
void setup() {
    PORTB &= ~(1<<PB4);
    DDRB |= (1<<PB4);
}

void loop() {
    delay(100);
    PORTB ^= (1<<PB4);
}
```

Assume a 16 MHz clock. Use PA4 (Mega D26), as in tutorial 14. The sketch polls Timer3 in CTC mode and toggles PA4 once per match. Each pair of matches makes one period, so at prescaler $N=8$,

$$OCR3A=\frac{16\,000\,000}{2\cdot 8\cdot 100}-1=9999.$$

A match occurs every 5 ms. The high and low intervals are each 5 ms, giving a nominal 100 Hz at 50% duty cycle. Timer setup and GPIO writes add small overhead.

```cpp
#define outPin 4  // PA4

void T3delay();

void setup() {
  PORTA &= ~(1<<outPin);
  DDRA |= (1<<outPin);
  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TCCR3A = 0x00;
  TIMSK3 = 0x00;
}

void loop()
{
  T3delay();
  PORTA ^= (1<<outPin);
}

void T3delay()
{
  OCR3AH = 0x27;
  OCR3AL = 0x0F;
  TCNT3H = 0x00;
  TCNT3L = 0x00;
  TIFR3 = (1<<OCF3A);  // Clear a pending flag by writing 1
  TCCR3B = 0x0A;        // CTC, clock / 8

  while ((TIFR3 & (1<<OCF3A)) == 0) {}
  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TIFR3 = (1<<OCF3A);
}
```

Use two switches from PC0 and PC1 to ground, with internal pull-ups enabled. Both open and both closed count as equal. Write ASCII `S` (0x53) for 00 or 11, and `D` (0x44) for 01 or 10. This is a parallel output at the PORTE latch, so keep other functions such as UART0 off those pins while testing.

```cpp
unsigned char z;

void setup() {
  DDRC &= ~0x03;
  PORTC |= 0x03;  // Pull-ups on PC1 and PC0
  DDRE = 0xFF;
}

void loop()
{
  z = PINC;
  z &= 0x03;
  switch (z) {
    case 0:
    case 3: PORTE = 'S'; break;
    case 1:
    case 2: PORTE = 'D'; break;
  }
}
```
